WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.530
Today’s guest video is from Grady at Practical
Engineering.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.540
He’s talking about a tragedy:

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.250
and the lessons we learned and the reasons
why it happened are fascinating.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.660
Also, he made his own props.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.950
Grady, take it away.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.710
In the summer of 1981, the Hyatt Regency Hotel
in Kansas City, Missouri,

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:21.450
hosted a large party in the multi-story atrium
area.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.450
During the event,

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:26.640
suspended walkways on the second and fourth
floors collapsed,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:31.550
killing over 100 people and injuring over
200 more.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:36.280
At the time, the accident was the worst structural
failure in US history.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.600
In the wake of the disaster,

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.980
investigators discovered a change to the original
design of the walkways

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:47.480
that was proposed by the fabricator and mistakenly
approved by the structural engineer.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.870
What, at first glance, seemed like a subtle
adjustment to the design,

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:55.379
turned out to be the root cause of the failure.

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:58.879
Two suspended walkways spanned the atrium
in the Hyatt Regency

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:03.539
with the second-floor walkway directly below
the fourth-floor walkway.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:06.260
Each was supported by a series of box girders

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.580
suspended by hanger rods
and retained by nuts and washers.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.950
The original design called for a single pair
of hanger rods,

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.830
which would pass through each fourth-floor
girder to the second-floor girder below.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.810
The fabricator responsible for constructing
the walkways objected to this plan

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.369
because it would require screw threads for
the entire length of the hanger rods,

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:29.930
which could easily be damaged during construction.

00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:34.220
So, they proposed to split the hanger rods
into two sets.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.979
One to connect the fourth-floor walkway to
the roof

00:01:36.979 --> 00:01:42.110
and one to connect the second-floor walkway
up to the fourth-floor girders.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:45.820
If you don’t notice the significance of
this change, you’re not alone.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.380
It was approved by the engineer without a
detailed review or calculations,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.189
which would have revealed its inherent flaw.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:56.110
In this setup, which represents the original
design,

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:01.110
the load of the two walkways is independently
transferred to the hanger rods.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.490
Notice how I can lift each girder without
affecting the other.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.680
Now, let’s look at the design change.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.510
In this configuration,

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.520
notice that the entire weight of the second-floor
walkway is being borne

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.780
not directly by the hanger rod,

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.640
but instead, by the girder above.

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.860
If I lift the fourth-floor walkway, the second-floor
walkway is lifted as well.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.270
The hanger rods are still carrying the same
load at the top,

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:32.830
but the two nuts on the upper girder are supporting
the weight of both walkways.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:38.020
This simple change effectively doubled the
load on those bolted connections.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.150
Imagine that you and a friend are both hanging
on a rope.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:46.220
The original design is the equivalent of you
both holding onto the rope independently,

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:51.760
whereas the design change is the equivalent
of your friend hanging onto your ankles.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:55.160
The total weight supported by the rope is
the same in both cases,

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.300
but your likelihood of maintaining a grip
is not.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:04.480
This subtle change was identified by investigators
as the primary cause of failure.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.970
With so many people on the walkways that evening,

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.710
the load on the connections was too great.

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:13.570
The box girders split open, slipping past
the washers and nuts

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.950
leading to the collapse of both walkways.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:22.020
There is an implicit handshake between a society
and its engineers.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.150
We hardly have a choice but to trust that
the constructed environment that we live in

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:28.420
is safe and sound.

00:03:28.420 --> 00:03:30.080
When an engineer seals a design,

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:36.120
he or she takes responsibility for its accuracy
and safety to the general public.

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:39.090
But the err is human and that includes engineers.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.320
So, we try to develop conventions and processes

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:46.730
that can catch and correct from mistakes before
they get too far.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.660
And that includes studying and learning from
errors made in the past.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:54.820
The failure of the Hyatt Regency walkways
is an important case study

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.980
taught to nearly every engineer

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.640
with the goal that such a tragedy will never
occur again.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.760
Practical Engineering is filled with great
videos, so go subscribe!

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.340
I would recommend starting with Grady’s
video on hydrostatics.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:08.340
Next time,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.780
a guest video with a prop that has been to
the moon.

